<title>LUXEMBOURG: EIB in bid to boost European film making.</title>
<headline>EIB in bid to boost European film making.</headline>
<dateline>LUXEMBOURG 1997-05-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>The European Investment Bank (EIB) said on Wednesday it was lending 71 million European curency units ($92 million) to PolyGram NV to help it produce European films.</p>
<p>ING Bank will underwrite the project, the EIB said.</p>
<p>&quot;This is the first time the EIB has financed the film industry,&quot; EIB president Sir Brian Unwin said in a statement.</p>
<p>&quot;We are doing so because the entertainment industry in Europe is making a growing contribution to the European Union's economy and is an important job creator,&quot; he said. &quot;Our purpose is to enhance its international competitiveness.</p>
<p>The EIB said the money would be used by PolyGram to shoot films prediminantly within the EU aimed at a family audience.</p>
<p>The bank is the EU's lending arm.</p>
</text>
